statement:
Fix a prime $\ell$ and a field $k$ of characteristic $p>0$ with $p\ne\ell$. Let $X,Y$ be separated schemes of finite type over $k$, and $f:X\to Y$ a separated finite-type $k$-morphism, in the standing setting [sk-def-sfb-dc-setting]. Let $\Lambda\in\{A,\mathbf Z_\ell,\mathcal O_E,\mathbf Q_\ell,E,\overline{\mathbf Q}_\ell\}$ be admissible: $A$ is a finite commutative ring killed by a power of $\ell$, and $E/\mathbf Q_\ell$ is finite with valuation ring $\mathcal O_E$. Use the condensed module-object coefficient categories and $\Dctf$ of [sk-def-sfb-constructible], and put $\mathcal T_Z=\Loc(\Dctf(Z,\Lambda))$ for $Z=X,Y$, as in [sk-def-localizing-closure]. All direct sums and compact generation below are relative to the fixed indexing universe [def-weil-size-convention; def-pc-compactly-generated]. Assume, as in [sk-lem-brown-hypotheses], that $\mathcal T_X$ is locally small and triangulated, has arbitrary direct sums and is compactly generated by a set of objects of $\Dctf(X,\Lambda)$.

For a chosen compactification $c=(X\xrightarrow{j}\overline X\xrightarrow{p}Y)$, where $j$ is a quasi-compact open immersion and $p$ proper [sk-def-nagata-sketch-data], write $Rf_!^c=Rp_*D(j_!)$ with the module-object convention of [sk-def-sfb-module-derived-lower-shriek], restricted to $\mathcal T_X\to\mathcal T_Y$. Then:

1. there is a functor $f_c^!:\mathcal T_Y\to\mathcal T_X$ and natural bijections $\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal T_Y}(Rf_!^c M,N)\simeq\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal T_X}(M,f_c^!N)$, for every $M\in\mathcal T_X$ and $N\in\mathcal T_Y$;
2. $f_c^!$ is exact, with its adjunction-induced coherent shift isomorphisms;
3. any two right adjoints of $Rf_!^c$ are isomorphic by a unique natural isomorphism compatible with the adjunctions. For different compactifications these adjoints are identified by the unique isomorphisms compatible with the canonical comparisons of $Rf_!$; these identifications satisfy the identity and cocycle conditions. Thus $f^!$ is independent, up to this unique compatible isomorphism, of the compactification.
